'''COCO''', officially the '''Concepción Organization of Cooperation''', also known as the '''Concepción Cooperative''', is an {{W|intergovernmental organization|intergovernmental}} {{W|Political union|political}} and {{W|economic union}} established in [[Concepción]], [[Balisca]], as part of the [[Concepción Accords]] that established security and military cooperation between Balisca and the [[Soviet Union]]. The organization has since been expanded to more nations, including [[New Duveland]] in 2025. The Cooperative is committed to the establishment of a cooperative system as an {{W|alter-globalization|alternative}} to the current system of {{W|corporate globalization}}, through the implementation of {{W|cooperative economics|cooperative economic ideals}}, such as {{W|mutualism}}, {{W|economic democracy}} and {{W|global justice movement|global justice}}.

Combined, the member states of COCO have an area of over 26 million square kilometers and a population of over 820 million. Their economies altogether are valued at over 34 trillion USD in terms of [[wikipedia:purchasing power parity|purchasing power parity]]. This makes COCO the second largest economy in the world, behind only that of {{W|China}}.

==History==

=== Initial years and Cooperative Treaty ===
{{Main|Cooperative Treaty Organization}}

=== Relationship with Comecon ===
{{See also|Comecon}}In its early decades, particularly from the 1950s to 1970s, CTO aligned strategically with [[W:COMECON|COMECON]] in opposition to [[W:Western imperialism|Western imperialism]], with both blocs seeking to create alternative trade, development, and industrial cooperation frameworks opposed to the [[United States|American]]-led [[W:Liberal international order|liberal international order]].

Joint agreements facilitated the exchange of industrial machinery, technical training, and infrastructural investment, particularly in energy, transport, and heavy industry. COMECON states—particularly the [[Soviet Union]], [[W:East Germany|East Germany]], and [[W:Czechoslovak Socialist Republic|Czechoslovakia]]—supported CTO development projects in Balisca and other member states. Despite early collaboration, CTO and COMECON's relationship began to diverge significantly by the late 1960s, as the [[W:Cold War|Cold War]] polarized international politics and both blocs hardened their internal doctrines. CTO’s emphasis on [[W:Worker self-management|worker self-management]]''',''' syndical federations''',''' and [[W:Decentralized planning|decentralized planning]] increasingly clashed with COMECON’s hierarchical''',''' state-driven economic model.

CTO officials often criticized the rigidity of COMECON’s structure, describing it as incompatible with bottom-up participatory planning and local autonomy. CTO’s decision-making was rooted in [[W:Horizontalidad|''horizontalidad'']] and communal sovereignty, which stood in contrast to COMECON’s [[W:Planned economy|centralized planning]] and Soviet political oversight. Baliscan delegates argued that COMECON’s system replicated many of the coercive features of capitalist globalization, albeit under a socialist veneer.

By the 1980s, the two blocs had institutionally decoupled, though individual member states continued bilateral cooperation. CTO members increasingly favored alternative models of [[W:Market socialism|market socialism]], mutualism, and cooperative development, focusing on horizontal South-South relations rather than bloc-aligned geopolitics. 

While CTO and COMECON ultimately diverged, their early cooperation laid the foundation for infrastructure, technical capacity, and transnational solidarity that would influence the later development of COCO. Many of the principles of non-alignment, resource sovereignty, and cooperative integration championed by CTO have found enduring expression in COCO’s post-Cold War platform. Presently, COCO’s supporters often cite the CTO–COMECON relationship as a historical example of the tensions between [[W:Authoritarian socialism|authoritarian]]/[[W:State socialism|state socialism]] and grassroots cooperative internationalism, reaffirming its commitment to the latter.

