==Membership and comparison==
{{Main|Member state of COCO}}

Through successive [[W:Enlargement|enlargements]], COCO and its predecessors have grown from the [[Inner Three|three founding states]] of the CTO to seven members. Countries accede to the union by becoming a party to the founding [[Treaties of COCO|treaties]], thereby subjecting themselves to the privileges and obligations of COCO membership. To become a member, a country must meet the [[Intramuros criteria]], defined at the inaugural meeting of the [[Cooperative Assembly]] in [[Concepción]]. These require a stable democracy that respects human rights and the [[W:Rule of law|rule of law]]; [[W:Economic democracy|economic democracy]]; and the acceptance of the obligations of membership, including cooperative guidelines. Evaluation of a country's fulfillment of the criteria is the responsibility of the [[Cooperative Economic Council|Cooperative Council]].

The five countries forming COCO+ are not members, but are considered partially aligned with the organization's economy and objectives: [[Formosa]], [[Okaiken]], the [[West Pacific]], the [[United Kingdom]] and [[Zambezi]]. These states are permanent invitees to COCO summits, are able to participate in policy discussions and organizational debates as non-voting members and are able to access funds for, and participate in, COCO's cooperative development initiatives.
